     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, for two reasons:\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.** 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6400 mg of ibuprofen a day.\n- The usual highest single oral dose for adults is 800 mg, so 1600 mg is double that.\n- The usual highest daily dose is 3200 mg, so this order is twice that.\n- Doses this high carry a serious risk of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and heart problems, even in a 70 kg adult with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n**2. Orders need a prescriber.** This system is for licensed clinicians. You said you're not one, so this needs to come from the patient's treating physician or another authorized prescriber. That keeps a clinical decision-maker accountable for the order.\n\n**What you can do instead:**\n- Ask the patient's attending physician or the medicine team to review what the patient needs. If the goal is pain or inflammation control, they can prescribe a suitable dose, such as 400\u2013800 mg every 6\u20138 hours, up to 3200 mg a day.\n- If you saw this dose written somewhere, like a note or a verbal request, raise it with the team or pharmacy. It may be a transcription error, and a patient-safety review might be warranted.\n\nIf a prescriber confirms an appropriate order, I can help them enter it."
      }
     ],
